          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em Jordan algebra
            is an algebra that may not be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} commutative
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, \cdot)
            any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            %em symmetrized product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi y
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \circ y
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  \\tfrac{1}{2}(x y + y x)
          %p
            makes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, \circ)
            a Jordan algebra. It is this relation that originally motivated the notion in discussion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanical
            system is what remains when one ignores the otherwise all-important
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutators"} commutators
            and hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            flows on observables.
          %p
            Later Jordan algebras were also studied for pure mathematical reasons, including their connection to self-dual convex homogeneous cones, hermitian symmetric spaces, 3-graded Lie algebras and the exceptional Lie algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔣
                      %mn 4
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔢
                      %mn 6
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{f}_4, \mathfrak{e}_6
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔢
                      %mn 7
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{e}_7
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+Jordan+algebras"} quadratic Jordan algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+triple+systems"} Jordan triple systems
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+pairs"} Jordan pairs
          %p
            More recently, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            associated to a noncommutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            was found to depend on the underlying Jordan algebra structure. See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} poset of commutative subalgebras
            for more on this.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong Jordan algebra
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonassociative+algebra"} nonassociative algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            satisfying the
            %strong Jordan identity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x x) (x y) = x ((x x) y)
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
          %p
            It follows (via a nontrivial argument) that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power-associative+algebra"} power-associative
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi m
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x^m y) x^n = x^m (y x^n)
          %p
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m, n \geq 1
            (and, trivially, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m, n \geq 0
            if there is an
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+element"} identity element
          %p
            Thus, we may equivalently define a Jordan algebra to be a commutative power-associative algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            such that for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in J
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^n
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \ge 1
            ) all commute with each other.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic"} characteristic
            is not
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            (or is any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            is invertible), then to any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            with product
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∘
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
            is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi y
                  %mover
                    %mo =
                    %mi def
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi y
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\circ y \stackrel{def}{=} \frac{x\cdot y + y \cdot x}{2}.
          %p
            Such Jordan algebras are called
            %strong special
            Jordan algebras; all others are called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Jordan+algebra"} exceptional
          %p
            The Jordan identity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x x) (x y) = x ((x x) y)
            may equivalently be restated as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{x^2} L_x = L_{x^2} L_x
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi J
                  %mo →
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_x \colon J \to J
            is the operation of left multiplication, or else, using commutativity, as a restricted form of the associative law:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x^2 y) x = x^2 (x y) \, .
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#frc Classification of formally real Jordan algebras
          %p
            %a{:href => "#JordanvNeumannWigner34"} Jordan, von Neumann &amp; Wigner 1934
            classified finite-dimensional formally real Jordan algebras.
          %p
            They began by defining an
            %strong ideal
            of a formally real Jordan algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            to be a linear subspace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq J
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in S
            implies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \circ y \in S
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in J
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            to be
            %strong simple
            when its only ideals were
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            itself. Then they proved that any finite-dimensional formally Jordan algebra is a direct sum of simple ones.
          %p
            This reduced the classification problem to the task of classifying
            %em simple
            finite-dimensional formally real Jordan algebras. There are four families of these, and one exception:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ×
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \times n
                self-adjoint real matrices,
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝔥
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{R})
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %mn 1
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                        %mo +
                        %mi b
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \circ b = {1\over 2}(a b + b a)
            %li
              %p
                The
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ×
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \times n
                self-adjoint complex matrices,
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝔥
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{C})
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %mn 1
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                        %mo +
                        %mi b
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \circ b = {1\over 2}(a b + b a)
            %li
              %p
                The
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ×
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \times n
                self-adjoint quaternionic matrices,
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝔥
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ℍ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{H})
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %mn 1
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                        %mo +
                        %mi b
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \circ b = {1\over 2}(a b + b a)
            %li
              %p
                The
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ×
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \times n
                self-adjoint octonionic matrices,
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝔥
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝕆
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{O})
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %mn 1
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                        %mo +
                        %mi b
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \circ b = {1\over 2}(a b + b a)
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≤
                        %mn 3
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \le 3
            %li
              %p
                The space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n \oplus \mathbb{R}
                with the product
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ,
                      %mi t
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mi t
                      %mo ′
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mi t
                      %mi t
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,t) \circ (x', t') = (t x' + t' x, x \cdot x' + t t').
          %p
            Here we say a square matrix with entries in the
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is
            %strong hermitian
            if it equals its conjugate transpose. (Note that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℍ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕆
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O}
            are all
            = succeed "-algebras.)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
          %p
            Because the octonions are an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alternative+algebra"} alternative algebra
            but not associative, we cannot go beyond
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3 \times 3
            matrices and still get a Jordan algebra. The
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \times 1
            self-adjoint octonionic matrices are just the real numbers, and the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \times 2
            ones are isomorphic to the spin factor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 9
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^9 \oplus \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3 \times 3
            self-adjoint octonionic matrices form the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+algebra"} Albert algebra
          %p
            Jordan algebras in the fifth family are called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong spin factors
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The Jordan algebra of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \times 2
                self-adjoint real matrices is isomorphic to the spin factor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2 \oplus \mathbb{R}
            %li
              %p
                The Jordan algebra of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \times 2
                self-adjoint complex matrices is isomorphic to the spin factor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 3
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3 \oplus \mathbb{R}
            %li
              %p
                The Jordan algebra of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \times 2
                self-adjoint quaternionic matrices is isomorphic to the spin factor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 5
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^5 \oplus \mathbb{R}
            %li
              %p
                The Jordan algebra of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \times 2
                self-adjoint octonionic matrices is isomorphic to the spin factor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 9
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^9 \oplus \mathbb{R}
          %p
            Because the spin factor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n \oplus \mathbb{R}
            can be identified with
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+algebras+and+supersymmetry"} division algebras and supersymmetry
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
                , The octonions, Sec. 3.3:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕆
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O}P^1
                and Lorentzian geometry. (
                %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/octonions/node11.html"}> web
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Huerta"} John Huerta
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0909.0551"} arXiv
          %p
            In 1983, Zelmanov drastically generalized the result of Jordan, von Neumann and Wigner by classifying all simple Jordan algebras, including infinite-dimensional ones (
            %a{:href => "#Zelmanov83"}> Zelmanov 83
            ).
          %h3#exceptional_jordan_algebras Exceptional Jordan algebras
          %p
            Among the exceptional Jordan algebras over the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 27
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 27
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+algebra"} Albert algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔸𝕝
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Al}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-adjoint+matrix"} self-adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3\times 3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix"} matrices
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonion"} octonions
            with the same formula as above for the product in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+product"} matrix product
            %em not
            form associative algebras, but only
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alternative+algebras"} alternative algebras
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#OriginInQuantumPhysics Origin in quantum physics
          %p
            Jordan algebras had their origin in the study of the foundations of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            that an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+quantum+observables"} algebra of quantum observables
            should satisfy.
          %p
            The unadorned phrase ‘algebra’ usually signals an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            are closed under addition and multiplication by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real
            scalars. In classical mechanics we can also multiply observables, but in quantum mechanics this becomes problematic. After all, given two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+linear+operator"} bounded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-adjoint"} self-adjoint
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operators"} linear operators
            on a complex
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
          %p
            However, in quantum mechanics one can still raise an observable to a power and obtain another observable. From squaring and taking real linear combinations, one can construct a commutative product using the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization+identity"} polarization identity
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi y
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \circ y
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  \\frac{1}{2}((x+y)^2 - x^2 - y^2)
                  \\;=\;
                  \\frac{1}{2}(x y + y x)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is sometimes called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-commutator"} anti-commutator
            (or more precisely: half the anti-commutator). Notice that it is analogous to the more famous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutator"} commutator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mo −
                  %mi y
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [x,y] \coloneqq x y - y x
          %p and that both together recover the full algebra of observables in that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo +
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x y = \frac{1}{2}[x,y] + x \circ y
          %p
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/JLB-algebra"} JLB-algebra
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
            equipped with the compatible structures of both a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            and a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star-algebra"} algebras
            in just this way, using
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∘
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                  %mo ≔
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\bullet} \coloneqq \frac{1}{2} \mathrm{i} [{-},{-}]
            as the operations.)
          %p
            From the point of view of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifolds"} Poisson manifolds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\{-,-\})
            breaks up into two pieces:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{-,-\}
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X)
                deforms to the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutator"} commutator
            %li
              %p
                the pointwise multiplication on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X)
                deforms into the Jordan algebra structure.
          %p
            This perspective on deformation quantization making the role of Jordan algebras explicit is mentioned for instance in (
            %a{:href => "#BatesWeinstein"}> Bates-Weinstein, p. 80
            ).
          %p
            The symmetrized product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-) \circ (-)
            is not associative, in general, but it is power-associative: any way of parenthesizing a product of copies of the same observable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            gives the same result. This led Jordan to define what is now called a
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong formally real Jordan algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            satisfying
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1^2 + \cdots + x_n^2 = 0 \quad \implies \quad x_1 = \cdots = x_n = 0
          %p
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+real+algebra"} formally real algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            a partial ordering: if we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \le y
            when the element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo −
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y - x
            is a sum of squares, it says that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi &amp;
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \le y \; \&amp; \; y \le x \quad \implies \quad x = y\,.
          %p So, in a formally real Jordan algebra we can reasonably talk about one observable being ‘greater’ than another.
          %p
            In fact the Jordan identity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \circ y) \circ (x \circ x) = x \circ (y \circ (x \circ x))
            is a
            %em consequence
            of the above definition of formally real Jordan algebra. So, every formally real Jordan algebra is a Jordan algebra (but not conversely).
          %p
            For more on this see also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "order-theoretic+structure+in+quantum+mechanics#RelationToTheNonCommutativePhaseSpace"} order-theoretic structure in quantum mechanics – Relation to non-commutative geometry
          %h3#relation_to_selfdual_homogeneous_convex_cones Relation to self-dual homogeneous convex cones
          %p
            Despite their origin in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            (
            %a{:href => "#OriginInQuantumPhysics"}> above
            ), the formalism of Jordan algebras may seem rather removed from the actual practice of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            and forms their Jordan product
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo +
                    %mi b
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {1\over 2}(a b + b a)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#OriginInQuantumPhysics"} previous section
            %em squaring
            an observable, which is something we actually do. But still, one must ask: can we see the classification of finite-dimensional formally real Jordan algebras, and thus the special role of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+division+algebras"} normed division algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            more closely tied to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+theory"} quantum theory
            as physicists usually practice it?
          %p
            One answer involves the Koecher–Vinberg classification of self-dual homogeneous
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                convex cones
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/convex+cones"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+theory"} quantum theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
            has the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            are described by self-adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \times n
            complex
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix"} matrices
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔥
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{C})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            1 also play another role. They are called
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+matrix"} density matrices
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
            of our quantum system: not just
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+states"} pure states
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+states"} mixed states
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔥
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho \in \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{C})
            allows us to define
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+value"} expectation value
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔥
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{C})
            via
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ρ
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle a \rangle = \tr(\rho a)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The map sending observables to their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+values"} expectation values
            is real-linear. The fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            is nonnegative is equivalent to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  a \ge 0 \; \implies \; \langle a \rangle \ge 0
          %p
            and the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            has trace 1 is equivalent to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle 1 \rangle = 1
                  \\,.
          %p
            All of this generalizes to an arbitrary finite-dimensional formally real Jordan algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity"} identity
            element. This lets us define a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+functional"} linear functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo :
                  %mi J
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \cdot \rangle : J \to \mathbb{R}
            that is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong> nonnegative
                \:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      a \ge 0  \implies \langle a \rangle \ge 0
            %li
              %p
                and
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong normalized
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\langle 1 \rangle = 1
                      \\,.
          %p
            But in fact, there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijective
            correspondence between linear functionals on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            and elements of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tr
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi J
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathrm{tr} \colon J \to \mathbb{R}
          %p
            defined so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{tr}(a)
            is the trace of the linear operator ‘multiplication by
            = succeed "\u2019." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle a, b \rangle = \mathrm{tr}(a \circ b)
          %p
            is a real-valued
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            is a finite-dimensional formally real Jordan algebra, any linear functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi J
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \cdot \rangle \colon J \to \mathbb{R}
            can be written as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle a \rangle = \mathrm{tr}(\rho \circ a)
          %p
            for a unique element
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho \in J
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho \in J
            gives a linear functional by this formula. While not obvious, it is true that the linear functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \cdot \rangle
            is nonnegative if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho \ge 0
            in terms of the ordering on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \cdot \rangle
            is normalized if and only if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{tr}(\rho) = 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho \in J
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho \ge 0
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{tr}(\rho) = 1
          %p
            These ideas help motivate an important theorem of Koecher and Vinberg. The idea is to axiomatize the situation we we have just described, in a way that does not mention the Jordan product in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
                between
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
                and its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+vector+space"} dual space
            %li
              %p
                the fact that ‘positive’ elements of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
                form a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone"} cone
          %p
            To find appropriate axioms, suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            is a finite-dimensional formally real Jordan algebra. Then seven facts are always true.
          %ol
            %li
              %p The set of positive observables
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi a
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      C = \{a \in A \colon a \gt 0\}
                      \\,.
              %p
                is a
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone"} cone
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in C
                implies that every positive multiple of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                is also in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p
                This cone is
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+space"} convex
              %p
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in C
                then any linear combination
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi a
                      %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x a + (1-x) b
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \le x \le 1
                also lies in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p It is an open set.
            %li
              %p
                It is
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong regular
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -a
                are both in the closure
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi C
                          %mo ¯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{C}
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = 0
              %p This condition may seem obscure, but if we note that
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi J
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\overline{C} = \{ a \in J \colon a \ge 0 \}
              %p
                we see that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                being regular simply means
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi and
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo −
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi a
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      a \ge 0 \; and \; -a \ge 0
                      \\quad \implies \quad
                      a = 0 ,
              %p a perfectly plausible assumption.
            %li
              %p
                Recall that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
                has an inner product; this is what lets us identify linear functionals on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
                with elements of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi J
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
                %strong dual cone
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mo *
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi J
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      C^* = \big\{
                      a \in J
                      \\colon
                      \\forall b \in A \; \; \langle a,b \rangle \gt 0
                      \\big\}
                      \\,,
              %p which one can check is indeed a cone.
              %p
                The fifth fact about
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is that it is
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong self-dual
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo =
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = C^*
              %p This formalizes the fact that states may be identified with special observables.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong homogeneous
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in C
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T : A \to A
                mapping
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                to itself in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijective
                way, with the property that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T a = b
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is highly symmetrical: no point of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is any ‘better’ than any other, at least if we only consider the linear structure of the space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p
                From another viewpoint, however, there is a very special point of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                of our Jordan algebra. And this brings us to our seventh and final fact: the cone
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is
                %strong pointed
                meaning that it is equipped with a distinguished element (in this case
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        1 \in
                        C
          %p
            In short: when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            is a finite-dimensional formally real Jordan algebra,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a pointed homogeneous self-dual regular open convex cone. All the elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in J
            are positive observables, but certain special ones, namely those with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle a, 1 \rangle = 1
          %p In fact, there is a category of pointed homogeneous self-dual regular open convex cones, where:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                An object is a finite-dimensional real inner product space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                equipped with a pointed homogeneous self-dual regular open convex cone
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \subset V
            %li
              %p
                A morphism from one object, say
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo ,
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,C)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ,
                        %mi C
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V',C')
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo :
                      %mi V
                      %mo →
                      %mi V
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T : V \to V'
                preserving the inner product and mapping
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                into
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
          %p
            Now for the payoff. The work of Koecher and Vinberg, nicely explained in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Koecher1999"} Koecher 1999
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              of pointed homogeneous self-dual regular open convex cones is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
              to the category of finite-dimensional formally real Jordan algebras.
          %p This means that the theorem of Jordan, von Neumann and Wigner also classifies the pointed homogeneous self-dual regular convex cones!
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Every pointed homogeneous self-dual regular open convex cone is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
              to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              of those on this list:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the cone of positive elements in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝔥
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{R})
              %li
                %p
                  the cone of positive elements in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝔥
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ℂ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{C})
              %li
                %p
                  the cone of positive elements in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝔥
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ℍ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{H})
              %li
                %p
                  the cone of positive elements in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝔥
                            %mn 3
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝕆
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h}_3(\mathbb{O})
              %li
                %p
                  the future lightcone in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mi n
                          %mo ⊕
                          %mi ℝ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n \oplus \mathbb{R}
          %p
            Some of this deserves a bit of explanation. For
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℍ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K} = \mathbb{R}, \mathbb{C}, \mathbb{H}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔥
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \in \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{K})
            is
            %strong positive
            if and only if the corresponding operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T : \mathbb{K}^n \to \mathbb{K}^n
            has
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi T
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle v, T v \rangle
                  \\gt
                  0
          %p
            for all nonzero
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝕂
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in \mathbb{K}^n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔥
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝕆
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h}_3(\mathbb{O})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,t) \in \mathbb{R}^n \oplus \mathbb{R}
            lies in the
            %strong future lightcone
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \gt 0
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi t
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi x
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t^2 - x \cdot x \gt 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,C)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V',C')
            is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi V
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \oplus V'
            equipped with the cone
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi V
                  %mo ′
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C \oplus C'
                  \\;=\;
                  \\big\{
                  (v,v') \in V\oplus V'
                  \\;\vert\;
                  v \in C, v' \in C'
                  \\big\}
                  \\mathrlap{\,.}
          %p In short: finite-dimensional formally real Jordan algebras arise fairly naturally as observables starting from a formalism where nonnegative observables form a cone, as long as we insist on some properties of this cone.
          %h3#relation_to_lie_algebras Relation to Lie algebras
          %p
            There is a deep connection between Jordan algebras and
            = succeed "," do
              %strong 3-graded Lie algebras
            = succeed "-graded" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathfrak{g} = \mathfrak{g}_{-1} \oplus \mathfrak{g}_0 \oplus \mathfrak{g}_1
                  \\,.
          %p
            In fact the category of Jordan algebras is equivalent to a category of 3-graded Lie algebras equipped with extra structure and properties. As a result, I. L. Kantor said “There are no Jordan algebras; there are only Lie algebras” (as quoted by
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Kac2007"} Kac 2007
          %p
            More precisely, suppose we are given a 3-graded Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            over a field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            of characteristic zero, equipped with the following structure and properties:
          %p
            a) if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in \mathfrak{g}_0
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a,b] = 0
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in \mathfrak{g}_{-1}
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = 0
          %p
            b)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}_{-1}
            is equipped with an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo +
                  %mi k
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}_{-1}=[\mathfrak{g}_0, p]+k p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathfrak{g}_1, p]
            generates the Lie algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}_0
          %p
            Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}_1
            becomes a Jordan algebra with product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \circ b=[[p, a], b] \, .
          %p
            Moreover this construction defines an equivalence between the category of 3-graded Lie algebras with the above extra structure and properties and the category of Jordan algebras (
            %a{:href => "#Kac2007"}> Kac 2007
            ).
          %p
            There are many variants of this result, called
            %strong Tits-Kantor-Koecher constructions
            after three researchers who invented different versions (
            %a{:href => "#Tits1962"}> Tits 1962
            ), (
            %a{:href => "Kantor1966"}> Kantor 1966
            ), (
            %a{:href => "#Koecher1967"}> Koecher 1967
            ). These constructions seem to be more cleanly stated in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+triple+systems"} Jordan triple systems
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+pairs"} Jordan pairs
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#BarbierCoulembier2018"} Barbier and Coulembier 2018
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#CavenySmirnov11"} Caveny and Smirnov 2011
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+triple+system"} Jordan triple system
          %h3#relation_to_derivations Relation to derivations
          %p
            In ordinary quantum mechanics, in the special case where observables are described as elements of the Jordan algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔥
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{C})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔥
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(t) \in \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{C})
            using Heisenberg’s equation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi t
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi H
                  %mo ,
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{d}{d t} O(t) = -i [H, O(t)]
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔥
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \in \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{C})
            is a fixed element called the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            %a{:href => "#Townsend2016"} Townsend 2016
            noted a nice solution to this problem when
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 4
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H = -4i [X, Y]
                  \\,,
          %p
            for some
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔥
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y \in \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{C})
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [[X, Y], A] = -\frac{1}{4}(X, A, Y)
                  \\,,
          %p where
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi Y
                  %mo −
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (X, A, Y) = (X \circ A) \circ Y - X \circ (A \circ Y)
          %p
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associator"} associator
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,A,
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            with respect to the Jordan product on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔥
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h}_n(\mathbb{C})
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi t
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{d}{d t} O(t) = (X, O(t), Y)
                  \\,,
          %p so that it uses only operations available in a Jordan algebra - and an associator rather than a commutator.
          %p
            This raises the question of when a self-adjoint complex matrix
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            can be written as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 4
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -4i [X, Y]
            for self-adjoint matrices
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is traceless since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{su}(n)
            is a compact simple real Lie algebra, and every element of such a Lie algebra is a commutator (Akhiezer,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.02934"} arXiv
            %em any
            self-adjoint complex matrix
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mi λ
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H' + \lambda I
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H'
            is traceless, so writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 4
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H' = -4i[X,Y]
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi H
                  %mo ,
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi H
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mi λ
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi H
                  %mo ,
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 4
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [H, O(t)]
                  \=
                  [H' + \lambda I, O(t)]
                  \=
                  [H, O(t)]
                  \=
                  \-4i \big[ [X,Y], O(t) \big]
                  \=
                  i (X, O(t), Y)
                  \\,,
          %p so we can rewrite Heisenberg’s equation as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi t
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{d}{d t} O(t) = (X, O(t), Y)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Moreover, in
            %em any
            Jordan algebra, a pair of elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y
            determines a derivation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \cdot, Y)
            (
            %a{:href => "#Jacobson1968"} Jacobson 1968
            Sec. I.7). In the finite-dimensional case there is no difficulty with exponentiating a derivation to obtain an automorphism. Thus, for any elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y
            of a finite-dimensional Jordan algebra, the solution of the above equation always determines a one-parameter group of Jordan algebra automorphisms.
          %h3#relation_to_commutative_subalgebras Relation to commutative subalgebras
          %p
            For every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            there is its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semilattice+of+commutative+subalgebras"} semilattice of commutative subalgebras
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ComSub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ComSub(A)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} von Neumann algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_2
            = succeed "-subfactors," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra+factor"} von Neumann algebra factor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ComSub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi ComSub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ComSub(A) \to ComSub(B)
            correspond to isomorphisms between the corresponding Jordan algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi J
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_J \to B_J
          %p
            For more details see
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semilattice+of+commutative+subalgebras"} semilattice of commutative subalgebras
          %h3#identities Identities
          %p
            All Jordan algebras obey a host of nonobvious identities. Here are a few of the identities listed in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Jacobson1968"} Jacobson ‘68 Sec. I.7
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} Polarizing
            the quartic identity
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (x \circ x) \circ (x \circ y)
                  \\;=\;
                  x \circ \big( (x \circ x) \circ y\big)
          %p we obey a tetralinear identity that can be written in various ways. If the characteristic of the underlying field is not 2, it can be written as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [c \circ d,a,b] + [b \circ c,a,d] + [b \circ d,a,c] = 0
          %p where
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi c
                  %mo −
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a,b,c] = (a \circ b) \circ c - a \circ (b \circ c)
          %p
            is the so-called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong associator
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_a \colon A \to A
            is the operation of left multiplication by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [[L_a, L_b], L_c] = L_{[a,b,c]} \, .
          %p
            This identity implies that the vector space of linear maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_a
            is closed under the Lie triple product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [[-,-],-]
            and thus forms a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+triple+system"} Lie triple system
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [L_a,L_b] \colon A \to A
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivations"} derivations
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %strong derivation
            of a Jordan algebra is a linear map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \colon A \to A
            obeying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leibniz+law"} Leibniz law
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi b
                  %mo +
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta(a \circ b) = \delta(a) \circ b + a \circ \delta(b) \, .
          %p
            Jordan algebra derivations of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [L_a,L_b]
            are called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong inner derivations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [L_a, L_b](c) = a \circ (b \circ c) - b \circ (a \circ c) =
                  a \circ (c \circ b) - (a \circ c) \circ b = -[a, c, b]
          %p so
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [L_a, L_b] = -[a , - , b] \, .
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
