                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BFb0096285"} doi:10.1007/BFb0096285
            %li#Townsend2016
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Townsend"}> Paul Townsend
                \:
                %em The Jordan formulation of quantum mechanics: a review
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.09228"}> arXiv:1612.09228
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            More on the physical motivation for regarding any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+quantum+observables"} algebra of quantum observables
            as a Jordan algebra:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Jordan algebras
              %em Getting to the Bottom of Noether’s Theorem
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.14741"}> arXiv:2006.14741
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p For the original Tits-Kantor-Koecher constructions, see:
          %ul
            %li#Tits1962
              %p
                Jacques Tits,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Une classe d’algèbres de Lie en relation avec les algèbres de Jordan
            %li#Kantor1966
              %p
                I. L. Kantor,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Transitive differential groups and invariant connections in homogeneous spaces
            %li#Koecher1967
              %p
                M. Koecher,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Imbedding of Jordan algebras into Lie algebras
          %p
            More modern versions of the TKK construction relating
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+superalgebras"} Jordan superalgebras
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebras"} super Lie algebras
            also have results interesting in the “non-super” case. The work of Barbier and Coulembier compares many previous constructions, while Caveny and Smirnov take an explicitly functorial attitude:
          %ul
            %li#Kac2007
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+G.+Kac"}> Victor G. Kac
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
